summary:
I integrated a closed (k=+1) FLRW universe with radiation (photons plus three massless neutrinos), dust Ω_m=0.315, curvature and τ with V=V0[1+cos(τ/f)]. The integrator is my own Dormand–Prince 5(4) code. It uses the second-order dH/dt equation through H=0, and an exact pendulum average for τ once its oscillation is 1000 times faster than the expansion. I checked it against textbook closed solutions and against full no-averaging runs. All 96 grid histories (|Ω_k| 1e-2 to 1e-5, f 0.2–2 M̄_P, w0 −0.95 to −0.7) turn around and crunch. No negative energy is needed. τ leaves the hilltop, first reaches its minimum at a=1.3–19, then oscillates as dust (|<w>| ≤ 1.1e-7 at the turnaround), and curvature reverses the expansion:
- a_max=(Ω_m+Ω_d)/|Ω_k| (residual ≤ 8.8e-7);
- t_turn=(π/2)(Ω_m+Ω_d)/|Ω_k|^1.5/H0 (to ≤ 1.9e-4), with t_crunch=2t_turn (to ≤ 1.9e-4);
- a_max 102–1.36e7, t_turn 2.3e4–9.8e10 Gyr, R_max 4.5e3–1.9e10 Gpc;
- momentum scale minimum ã_min=1/a_max = 7.4e-8 to 9.8e-3, CMB at the turnaround 2e-7–0.027 K.

Representative case (|Ω_k|=1e-3, f=M̄_P, w0=−0.8): a_max 1726, t_turn 1.24e6 Gyr, R_max 2.43e5 Gpc, T 1.58 mK.

At fixed (w0, wa), d ln t_turn/d ln|Ω_k| = −1.488 to −1.505. So the record's over-constraint (f, τ_i) → (w0, wa, t_turn) fails for the written potential in a closed universe. With V0 cos(τ/f) the turnaround comes 10–42 Gyr from today and depends on curvature by at most 0.9%.

Conformal time: χ0 ≈ 3.15√|Ω_k|, χ_turn = π + (1.2–3.8)√|Ω_k|, χ_crunch = 2π + (−0.34 to 3.6)√|Ω_k|, depending on the oscillation phase. Light from the big bang reaches the antipode before the turnaround and closes the circuit near the crunch in most histories. Light emitted today goes all the way round only for f=2, w0=−0.95. The crunch is dominated by τ's kinetic energy (w=1). Flat and open universes never turn around: ã falls as t^-2/3 and t^-1.
